From u1fb54f491646a589 at groups.google.com  Thu Jun 28 02:57:12 2018
From: u1fb54f491646a589 at groups.google.com (Stephen Buckland)
Date: Thu, 28 Jun 2018 02:57:12 +0000
Subject: {Disarmed} RE: {Disarmed} [distance-sampling] TRANSECT vs DISTANCE capabilities
Message-ID: <B4XNGJ7PkAU.IMbc6EYqBQAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <B4XNGJ7PkAU.F4A3fQMlCgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
References: <B4XNGJ7PkAU.F4A3fQMlCgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/B4XNGJ7PkAU/m/IMbc6EYqBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Luke, that comment from the paper makes no sense at all.  It suggests the writer is completely ignorant of the issue of sampling variation!  TRANSECT probably would have quite happily modelled those data.  TRANSECT had only the Fourier series model (uniform key + cosine adjustments in Distance parlance).  It fitted the model by method of moments, while Distance fits using maximum likelihood.  Yes, Distance has much more flexibility, but no, it would not be right to say that it would model these data adequately, but TRANSECT could not.  The fault is not with TRANSECT but with the statement in the paper.

Steve Buckland

